/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Leviticus chapters 21-25
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("leviticus", [
{n:21, name:"Holiness of the Priests",
 seal:{ref:"Leviticus 21:8",verse:"Thou shalt sanctify him therefore; for he offereth the bread of thy God: he shall be holy unto thee: for I the LORD, which sanctify you, am holy."},
 facts:["Did you know the high priest could not even attend his own father's or mother's funeral? The anointing oil upon him set him wholly apart from the rites of death (Leviticus 21:11).","Did you know a priest with a physical blemish was not cast out of fellowship? He still ate the holy bread; he was only kept from approaching the altar (Leviticus 21:22).","Did you know priests were forbidden the common mourning rites of their day, like shaving the head or cutting the flesh? Their grief had to look different from the nations around them (Leviticus 21:5)."],
 questions:[
  {q:"To whom did the LORD command Moses to speak these laws of priestly holiness?", options:["The priests, the sons of Aaron","The elders of the tribes","All the Levites","The whole congregation of Israel"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:1",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses, Speak unto the priests the sons of Aaron, and say unto them, There shall none be defiled for the dead among his people:",
   insight:"Holiness began with the leaders. God set the standard highest for those who stood nearest to Him, a pattern that still shapes pastoral calling."},
  {q:"What was the standing rule for a priest regarding the dead?", options:["None of them was to be defiled for the dead among his people","They could mourn anyone except strangers","Only the high priest had to avoid the dead","They had to bury every dead Israelite"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:1",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses, Speak unto the priests the sons of Aaron, and say unto them, There shall none be defiled for the dead among his people:",
   insight:"Contact with death pictured the brokenness that holiness was set apart from. Priests embodied life in God's presence, so they kept their distance from death's defilement."},
  {q:"Which unmarried sister was an exception to the rule?", options:["His sister who was a virgin, near to him, and had no husband","His eldest sister, whatever her state","His sister who lived far away","His half sister by his father only"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:3",verse:"And for his sister a virgin, that is nigh unto him, which hath had no husband; for her may he be defiled.",
   insight:"God carved out room for genuine family grief. Holiness was never meant to harden the heart against those nearest to us."},
  {q:"Which mourning customs were forbidden to the priests?", options:["Making baldness on the head, shaving the corner of the beard, or cutting the flesh","Wearing sackcloth and sitting in ashes","Weeping aloud at the burial","Fasting for seven days"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:5",verse:"They shall not make baldness upon their head, neither shall they shave off the corner of their beard, nor make any cuttings in their flesh.",
   insight:"Pagan grief marked the body in despair, as if death had the last word. God's priests were to grieve with hope, not with the hopeless rites of the nations."},
  {q:"Why were the priests to be holy and not profane God's name?", options:["Because they offered the offerings made by fire, the bread of their God","Because they were the oldest men of each tribe","Because they guarded the temple treasury","Because they alone could read the law"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:6",verse:"They shall be holy unto their God, and not profane the name of their God: for the offerings of the LORD made by fire, and the bread of their God, they do offer: therefore they shall be holy.",
   insight:"Privilege carried weight. Handling holy things meant becoming holy, because what we repeatedly do before God shapes who we are."},
  {q:"What kind of woman might a priest not marry?", options:["A woman put away from her husband, that is, divorced","A widow of a fellow priest","A daughter of a Levite","A woman from another tribe of Israel"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:7",verse:"They shall not take a wife that is a whore, or profane; neither shall they take a woman put away from her husband: for he is holy unto his God.",
   insight:"The priest's home was to mirror the covenant faithfulness he served. His marriage was part of his ministry, not separate from it."},
  {q:"What was the congregation commanded to do regarding the priest?", options:["Sanctify him, for he offers the bread of God","Pay him double the wages of a Levite","Build him a house beside the sanctuary","Exempt his sons from service"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:8",verse:"Thou shalt sanctify him therefore; for he offereth the bread of thy God: he shall be holy unto thee: for I the LORD, which sanctify you, am holy.",
   insight:"The people shared responsibility for the priest's holiness. We still have a part in honoring and upholding those who serve God among us."},
  {q:"What was the penalty for a priest's daughter who profaned herself by harlotry?", options:["She was to be burnt with fire","She was cut off from the priesthood alone","She paid a fine of fifty shekels","She was sent out of the camp"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:9",verse:"And the daughter of any priest, if she profane herself by playing the whore, she profaneth her father: she shall be burnt with fire.",
   insight:"The severity shows how seriously God took the witness of a priestly family. A leader's household either adorns the message or betrays it."},
  {q:"In mourning, what two things must the high priest never do?", options:["Uncover his head or tear his clothes","Wash his hands or trim his beard","Leave the city or speak to mourners","Eat bread or drink wine"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:10",verse:"And he that is the high priest among his brethren, upon whose head the anointing oil was poured, and that is consecrated to put on the garments, shall not uncover his head, nor rend his clothes;",
   insight:"The high priest carried the nation's grief before God, yet his consecration could not be laid aside even in sorrow. Calling outlasts our crises."},
  {q:"For which close relatives could even the high priest not defile himself with the dead?", options:["Neither for his father nor for his mother","Neither for his wife nor his children","Neither for his brother nor his sister","Neither for his sons nor his daughters"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:11",verse:"Neither shall he go in to any dead body, nor defile himself for his father, or for his mother;",
   insight:"No exception remained for the high priest. The closer the office to God's presence, the more complete the separation from death."},
  {q:"Why could the high priest not leave the sanctuary?", options:["Because the crown of the anointing oil of his God was upon him","Because the veil had been closed behind him","Because the people needed him at the gate","Because the Sabbath had begun"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:12",verse:"Neither shall he go out of the sanctuary, nor profane the sanctuary of his God; for the crown of the anointing oil of his God is upon him: I am the LORD.",
   insight:"Anointing was a crown he wore into every duty. What God consecrates, He also keeps, and the servant stays at his post."},
  {q:"What kind of wife was the high priest commanded to take?", options:["A wife in her virginity","A widow of a former high priest","The daughter of any priest","A woman of his own choosing"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:13",verse:"And he shall take a wife in her virginity.",
   insight:"The highest office carried the strictest standard in marriage. Closeness with God shaped closeness at home."},
  {q:"Which women were forbidden to the high priest?", options:["A widow, a divorced woman, a profane woman, or a harlot","A barren woman or a foreigner","A woman older than himself","A woman from the tribe of Dan"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:14",verse:"A widow, or a divorced woman, or profane, or an harlot, these shall he not take: but he shall take a virgin of his own people to wife.",
   insight:"Every restriction guarded the same treasure: an undivided household for an undivided devotion. God cares about the marriages of His servants."},
  {q:"What was at stake in the high priest's choice of a wife?", options:["That he not profane his seed among his people","That his sons learn the priestly crafts","That his dowry enrich the sanctuary","That the people approve his wedding"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:15",verse:"Neither shall he profane his seed among his people: for I the LORD do sanctify him.",
   insight:"Holiness was meant to be inherited, passed from one generation to the next. A godly line begins with a guarded marriage."},
  {q:"Who was barred from approaching to offer the bread of God?", options:["Any priest of Aaron's seed who had a blemish","Any priest under thirty years old","Any priest who had touched the dead","Any priest born outside the camp"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:17",verse:"Speak unto Aaron, saying, Whosoever he be of thy seed in their generations that hath any blemish, let him not approach to offer the bread of his God.",
   insight:"The blemish rule pictured a greater truth: only what is whole and unflawed can stand before a holy God, which is why we needed a perfect High Priest."},
  {q:"Which condition is named as a blemish barring altar service?", options:["Blindness","Gray hair","A loud voice","Left-handedness"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:18",verse:"For whatsoever man he be that hath a blemish, he shall not approach: a blind man, or a lame, or he that hath a flat nose, or any thing superfluous,",
   insight:"God listed the blemishes plainly so no one would guess. His standards are clear, not riddles, and clarity is a mercy."},
  {q:"Though he could eat the holy bread, where could the blemished priest not go?", options:["He could not go in to the veil nor come near the altar","He could not enter the camp's gate","He could not teach the law to the people","He could not offer the morning sacrifice alone"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:21",verse:"No man that hath a blemish of the seed of Aaron the priest shall come nigh to offer the offerings of the LORD made by fire: he hath a blemish; he shall not come nigh to offer the bread of his God.",
   insight:"Grace made room for him at the table while guarding the altar. God is both welcoming and holy, and both truths stand together."},
  {q:"What privilege remained for the priest with a blemish?", options:["He could still eat the bread of his God, both most holy and holy","He could still enter the most holy place yearly","He could still offer the evening lamb","He could still bless the congregation"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:22",verse:"He shall eat the bread of his God, both of the most holy, and of the holy.",
   insight:"A limitation in one duty did not mean rejection from fellowship. God still fed him at His own table."},
  {q:"Why was the blemished priest kept from the sanctuary and altar?", options:["So that he would not profane God's sanctuaries","So the people would not pity him","So the other priests would not stumble","So the sacrifices would cost less"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:23",verse:"Only he shall not go in unto the vail, nor come nigh unto the altar, because he hath a blemish; that he profane not my sanctuaries: for I the LORD do sanctify them.",
   insight:"The restriction protected God's holiness, not the priest's dignity. Worship is about God's worth, not our performance."},
  {q:"To whom did Moses deliver all these holiness laws?", options:["To Aaron, his sons, and all the children of Israel","To the seventy elders alone","To Aaron only, in private","To the Levites at the tabernacle door"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 21:24",verse:"And Moses told it unto Aaron, and to his sons, and unto all the children of Israel.",
   insight:"These were not secret priestly codes. The whole congregation heard them, because the people's reverence upheld the priests' holiness."}
 ]},
{n:22, name:"Holy Things and Offerings",
 seal:{ref:"Leviticus 22:31",verse:"Therefore shall ye keep my commandments, and do them: I am the LORD."},
 facts:["Did you know a priest's hired servant could not eat the holy food, but a servant bought with the priest's own money could? Belonging, not labor, set the table (Leviticus 22:10-11).","Did you know someone who ate a holy thing by mistake had to repay it plus a fifth, a twenty percent penalty for carelessness with sacred things (Leviticus 22:14)?","Did you know a mother animal and her young could not be killed on the same day? Mercy reached even into the laws of sacrifice (Leviticus 22:28)."],
 questions:[
  {q:"What charge was given to Aaron and his sons about the holy things?", options:["That they not profane God's holy name in the things hallowed to Him","That they eat them only at Passover","That they wash before every meal","That they count them each month"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:2",verse:"Speak unto Aaron and to his sons, that they separate themselves from the holy things of the children of Israel, and that they profane not my holy name in those things which they hallow unto me: I am the LORD.",
   insight:"Holy things belonged to a holy name. Carelessness with what is consecrated is carelessness with God Himself."},
  {q:"What befell an unclean person who approached the holy things?", options:["That soul would be cut off from God's presence","He would be fined a lamb","He would serve a month outside the camp","He would wash seven times"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:3",verse:"Say unto them, Whosoever he be of all your seed among your generations, that goeth unto the holy things, which the children of Israel hallow unto the LORD, having his uncleanness upon him, that soul shall be cut off from my presence: I am the LORD.",
   insight:"Approaching God on our own terms ends in separation. Purity was the doorway, and God guarded it."},
  {q:"Which conditions kept a priest from eating the holy things until cleansed?", options:["Leprosy or a running issue","Blindness or lameness","Old age or childhood","Hunger or thirst"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:4",verse:"What man soever of the seed of Aaron is a leper, or hath a running issue; he shall not eat of the holy things, until he be clean. And whoso toucheth any thing that is unclean by the dead, or a man whose seed goeth from him;",
   insight:"God paused even the priests' privileges until they were clean. No office exempts anyone from dealing with uncleanness."},
  {q:"Touching what made a priest unclean?", options:["Anything unclean by the dead","The altar of burnt offering","The table of showbread","The bronze laver"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:4",verse:"What man soever of the seed of Aaron is a leper, or hath a running issue; he shall not eat of the holy things, until he be clean. And whoso toucheth any thing that is unclean by the dead, or a man whose seed goeth from him;",
   insight:"Death's uncleanness spread by touch, a picture of how sin contaminates. Holiness required vigilance about contact."},
  {q:"After washing, when could the unclean priest eat the holy things again?", options:["When the sun had gone down","After seven full days","At the next new moon","When the priest offered a lamb"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:7",verse:"And when the sun is down, he shall be clean, and shall afterward eat of the holy things; because it is his food.",
   insight:"Cleansing had a completion point: sunset. God gave a clear moment when the unclean became clean again, and grace always names the way back."},
  {q:"What meat might a priest never eat?", options:["An animal that died of itself or was torn by beasts","The shoulder of the peace offering","Meat cooked with leaven","The Passover lamb's leftovers"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:8",verse:"That which dieth of itself, or is torn with beasts, he shall not eat to defile himself therewith: I am the LORD.",
   insight:"What died on its own carried unknown corruption. God's servants were to be careful even about their tables."},
  {q:"What warning came with keeping the priestly ordinance?", options:["Lest they bear sin for it and die for profaning it","Lest the people stop bringing offerings","Lest the Levites complain","Lest the altar fire go out"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:9",verse:"They shall therefore keep mine ordinance, lest they bear sin for it, and die therefore, if they profane it: I the LORD do sanctify them.",
   insight:"Neglect had consequences, and God said so plainly. Warnings are love spoken in advance."},
  {q:"Who was barred from eating the holy things?", options:["Any stranger, an outsider","Any Levite under age","Any priest's daughter","Any hired Israelite"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:10",verse:"There shall no stranger eat of the holy thing: a sojourner of the priest, or an hired servant, shall not eat of the holy thing.",
   insight:"The holy food marked belonging to the priestly household. Some tables in Scripture are fenced, not because God is stingy, but because holiness is real."},
  {q:"Which two persons in the priest's household could not eat the holy things?", options:["A sojourner and a hired servant","A widow and an orphan","A Levite and his son","A guest and a traveler"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:10",verse:"There shall no stranger eat of the holy thing: a sojourner of the priest, or an hired servant, shall not eat of the holy thing.",
   insight:"Residence was not the same as belonging. God distinguished between those joined to the house and those merely passing through."},
  {q:"Which servant of the priest was permitted to eat the holy things?", options:["One bought with the priest's own money","One hired for the harvest season","One lent by a neighbor","One working off a debt"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:11",verse:"But if the priest buy any soul with his money, he shall eat of it, and he that is born in his house: they shall eat of his meat.",
   insight:"Purchase meant permanent belonging to the household. Those truly bought into the family shared the family's bread."},
  {q:"What did the priest's daughter lose by marrying an outsider?", options:["The right to eat the holy offerings","Her share of the tithe","Her place in the camp","Her father's blessing"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:12",verse:"If the priest’s daughter also be married unto a stranger, she may not eat of an offering of the holy things.",
   insight:"Marriage joined her to another household, and its table, with it. Covenant choices carry covenant consequences."},
  {q:"When could the priest's daughter eat the holy food again?", options:["If widowed or divorced, childless, and returned to her father's house","After seven years had passed","If she brought a sin offering","When her father died"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:13",verse:"But if the priest’s daughter be a widow, or divorced, and have no child, and is returned unto her father’s house, as in her youth, she shall eat of her father’s meat: but there shall no stranger eat thereof.",
   insight:"God left the door open for her return. The father's house still had a seat for the daughter who came home."},
  {q:"What must be added by one who ate a holy thing unknowingly?", options:["A fifth part, given to the priest with the holy thing","A tenth part, given to the Levites","A double portion, burnt on the altar","Nothing, since it was a mistake"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:14",verse:"And if a man eat of the holy thing unwittingly, then he shall put the fifth part thereof unto it, and shall give it unto the priest with the holy thing.",
   insight:"Even accidental wrongs against holy things required amends. Ignorance lessened guilt but did not erase responsibility."},
  {q:"What standard did an acceptable burnt offering have to meet?", options:["It had to be perfect, without blemish","It had to be the largest of the flock","It had to be a year old exactly","It had to be white in color"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:19",verse:"Ye shall offer at your own will a male without blemish, of the beeves, of the sheep, or of the goats.",
   insight:"God deserved the best, not the leftovers. The unblemished animal preached that worship costs us our finest."},
  {q:"Why was a blemished animal refused from the altar?", options:["It would not be acceptable","It would defile the fire","It would anger the priests","It would spoil the meat"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:20",verse:"But whatsoever hath a blemish, that shall ye not offer: for it shall not be acceptable for you.",
   insight:"God measures acceptability, not our convenience. Bringing Him our damaged goods and calling it devotion is what He calls contempt."},
  {q:"Which defect disqualified an animal from the altar?", options:["Blindness","A torn ear from birth","A limp from old age","A coat of mixed colors"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:22",verse:"Blind, or broken, or maimed, or having a wen, or scurvy, or scabbed, ye shall not offer these unto the LORD, nor make an offering by fire of them upon the altar unto the LORD.",
   insight:"God named blindness, brokenness, and disease as unfit. He wanted Israel to learn that He is worthy of wholeness."},
  {q:"For what offering could an animal with a minor defect be used?", options:["A freewill offering, but never for a vow","A sin offering, but never for peace","A burnt offering on feast days","Any offering except the Passover"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:23",verse:"Either a bullock or a lamb that hath any thing superfluous or lacking in his parts, that mayest thou offer for a freewill offering; but for a vow it shall not be accepted.",
   insight:"God made room for joyful freewill gifts while guarding vowed worship. Generosity has latitude; promises have precision."},
  {q:"Which injured animals were forbidden on the altar?", options:["Those bruised, crushed, broken, or cut","Those bitten by serpents","Those born in the winter","Those raised by strangers"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:24",verse:"Ye shall not offer unto the LORD that which is bruised, or crushed, or broken, or cut; neither shall ye make any offering thereof in your land.",
   insight:"Mutilated offerings pictured a mutilated devotion. God refused worship that came to Him already damaged."},
  {q:"How long must a newborn calf, lamb, or kid stay with its mother before being offered?", options:["Seven days, accepted from the eighth day onward","Three days, accepted on the fourth","Forty days, like a purification","One full month"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:27",verse:"When a bullock, or a sheep, or a goat, is brought forth, then it shall be seven days under the dam; and from the eighth day and thenceforth it shall be accepted for an offering made by fire unto the LORD.",
   insight:"Even in sacrifice, God honored the bond of mother and young. His law carried tenderness inside its strictness."},
  {q:"What was forbidden regarding a cow or ewe and her young?", options:["Killing both mother and young on the same day","Selling them to different owners","Offering them in the same year","Naming them after the feast"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 22:28",verse:"And whether it be cow or ewe, ye shall not kill it and her young both in one day.",
   insight:"This small mercy law revealed a large-hearted God. If He guards an animal's bond, how much more does He guard ours?"}
 ]},
{n:23, name:"The Appointed Feasts",
 seal:{ref:"Leviticus 23:3",verse:"Six days shall work be done: but the seventh day is the sabbath of rest, an holy convocation; ye shall do no work therein: it is the sabbath of the LORD in all your dwellings."},
 facts:["Did you know the Feast of Weeks required bread baked WITH leaven, the one offering where leaven was commanded rather than banned (Leviticus 23:17)?","Did you know the Day of Atonement was measured evening to evening, the pattern behind the Jewish day beginning at sunset (Leviticus 23:32)?","Did you know the Feast of Booths was a week of dwelling in booths so later generations would remember how God sheltered Israel after Egypt (Leviticus 23:43)?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"What were the appointed feasts of the LORD called?", options:["Holy convocations","Solemn fasts","Joyful dances","Sacred marches"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:2",verse:"Speak unto the children of Israel, and say unto them, Concerning the feasts of the LORD, which ye shall proclaim to be holy convocations, even these are my feasts.",
   insight:"God's feasts were gatherings, not private devotions. Faith was meant to be celebrated together, in holy assembly."},
  {q:"What marked the seventh day of each week?", options:["A sabbath of rest, a holy convocation, with no work","A day of extra sacrifices only","A fast from morning to evening","A day for washing the tabernacle"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:3",verse:"Six days shall work be done: but the seventh day is the sabbath of rest, an holy convocation; ye shall do no work therein: it is the sabbath of the LORD in all your dwellings.",
   insight:"Rhythm was God's idea: six days of work, one of rest. Rest is not laziness; it is trust that God holds the world while we stop."},
  {q:"When was the LORD's Passover kept?", options:["The fourteenth day of the first month, at evening","The first day of the seventh month","The tenth day of the first month","The Sabbath nearest the spring"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:5",verse:"In the fourteenth day of the first month at even is the LORD’S passover.",
   insight:"Passover anchored Israel's calendar to redemption. God's people have always dated their lives from the day He delivered them."},
  {q:"When did the Feast of Unleavened Bread begin, and how long did it last?", options:["The fifteenth day of the first month, for seven days","The first day of the second month, for three days","The fourteenth day, for one day","The new moon of spring, for ten days"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:6",verse:"And on the fifteenth day of the same month is the feast of unleavened bread unto the LORD: seven days ye must eat unleavened bread.",
   insight:"A week without leaven taught in the body what the heart needed: the old life of Egypt had to be purged out completely."},
  {q:"What was brought to the priest at the beginning of the harvest?", options:["A sheaf of the firstfruits","The firstborn of every flock","A basket of summer fruit","Ten loaves of new bread"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:10",verse:"Speak unto the children of Israel, and say unto them, When ye be come into the land which I give unto you, and shall reap the harvest thereof, then ye shall bring a sheaf of the firstfruits of your harvest unto the priest:",
   insight:"The first sheaf went to God before the harvest was gathered. Firstfruits declare that all of it was His gift."},
  {q:"When was the wave sheaf waved before the LORD?", options:["On the day after the Sabbath","On the morning of Passover","At the new moon","On the seventh day of the feast"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:11",verse:"And he shall wave the sheaf before the LORD, to be accepted for you: on the morrow after the sabbath the priest shall wave it.",
   insight:"Resurrection morning echoes this day: the morrow after the Sabbath. God loves to begin new harvests the day after rest."},
  {q:"What animal was offered with the wave sheaf?", options:["A male lamb of the first year, without blemish","A young bullock of the herd","A kid of the goats","A turtledove from the cote"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:12",verse:"And ye shall offer that day when ye wave the sheaf an he lamb without blemish of the first year for a burnt offering unto the LORD.",
   insight:"Every harvest began at the altar, not the field. Gratitude that costs something is gratitude that lasts."},
  {q:"What were the grain and drink measures offered with the sheaf?", options:["Two tenth deals of flour with oil, and a fourth of a hin of wine","One tenth deal of flour and a full hin of oil","Three loaves and a cup of new wine","A handful of grain and a sip of wine"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:13",verse:"And the meat offering thereof shall be two tenth deals of fine flour mingled with oil, an offering made by fire unto the LORD for a sweet savour: and the drink offering thereof shall be of wine, the fourth part of an hin.",
   insight:"God prescribed generous portions, not tokens. Worship that is measured out carefully should still be measured generously."},
  {q:"What was forbidden until the firstfruits offering was brought?", options:["Eating bread, parched corn, or green ears of the new harvest","Drinking wine of the new vintage","Selling grain in the market","Baking bread for the Sabbath"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:14",verse:"And ye shall eat neither bread, nor parched corn, nor green ears, until the selfsame day that ye have brought an offering unto your God: it shall be a statute for ever throughout your generations in all your dwellings.",
   insight:"Israel tasted nothing new until God had His portion. Delaying gratification for God's glory trains the appetite of faith."},
  {q:"How long was the count from the wave sheaf to the next feast?", options:["Seven complete Sabbaths","Forty days","Three months","One full year"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:15",verse:"And ye shall count unto you from the morrow after the sabbath, from the day that ye brought the sheaf of the wave offering; seven sabbaths shall be complete:",
   insight:"God taught His people to count toward celebration. Anticipation is a spiritual discipline; the calendar itself preached hope."},
  {q:"What was offered on the fiftieth day?", options:["A new grain offering to the LORD","A second Passover lamb","The first wine of summer","A ram for each tribe"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:16",verse:"Even unto the morrow after the seventh sabbath shall ye number fifty days; and ye shall offer a new meat offering unto the LORD.",
   insight:"Fifty days after redemption's feast came the harvest feast. God's salvation always ripens into fruitfulness."},
  {q:"What was distinctive about the two wave loaves?", options:["They were baked with leaven","They were baked without any flour","They were twelve in number","They were eaten by the priests raw"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:17",verse:"Ye shall bring out of your habitations two wave loaves of two tenth deals: they shall be of fine flour; they shall be baken with leaven; they are the firstfruits unto the LORD.",
   insight:"Leaven, usually banned, was commanded here: a harvest offering from ordinary daily bread. God accepts our common work made holy."},
  {q:"Which animals formed the burnt offering at the Feast of Weeks?", options:["Seven lambs, one young bullock, and two rams","Ten bullocks and ten rams","One lamb for each tribe","Three goats and a bullock"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:18",verse:"And ye shall offer with the bread seven lambs without blemish of the first year, and one young bullock, and two rams: they shall be for a burnt offering unto the LORD, with their meat offering, and their drink offerings, even an offering made by fire, of sweet savour unto the LORD.",
   insight:"The scale of the offering matched the scale of the blessing. Big harvests called for big thanksgiving."},
  {q:"What were the sin and peace offerings at the Feast of Weeks?", options:["One kid of the goats for sin, and two lambs for peace","Two bullocks for sin, and one ram for peace","A lamb for sin, and a goat for peace","Seven doves for sin, and bread for peace"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:19",verse:"Then ye shall sacrifice one kid of the goats for a sin offering, and two lambs of the first year for a sacrifice of peace offerings.",
   insight:"Even at the happiest feast, sin was still atoned for. Joy and repentance belong together in God's presence."},
  {q:"Who benefited from the unharvested field corners and gleanings?", options:["The poor and the stranger","The priests and Levites","The widows of the tribe","The king's household"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:22",verse:"And when ye reap the harvest of your land, thou shalt not make clean riddance of the corners of thy field when thou reapest, neither shalt thou gather any gleaning of thy harvest: thou shalt leave them unto the poor, and to the stranger: I am the LORD your God.",
   insight:"God built generosity into the harvest itself. The corners of our fields, our margins, belong to the needy by divine design."},
  {q:"When was the memorial of blowing of trumpets?", options:["The first day of the seventh month","The tenth day of the first month","The last day of the harvest","The new moon of winter"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:24",verse:"Speak unto the children of Israel, saying, In the seventh month, in the first day of the month, shall ye have a sabbath, a memorial of blowing of trumpets, an holy convocation.",
   insight:"Trumpets woke the nation to attention. God uses loud reminders to rouse sleepy hearts before solemn days."},
  {q:"When was the Day of Atonement, and what did the people do?", options:["The tenth day of the seventh month; they afflicted their souls","The first day of the year; they feasted","The fifteenth day of the month; they danced","The Sabbath of Sabbaths; they slept"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:27",verse:"Also on the tenth day of this seventh month there shall be a day of atonement: it shall be an holy convocation unto you; and ye shall afflict your souls, and offer an offering made by fire unto the LORD.",
   insight:"One day a year, the whole nation humbled itself together. Corporate repentance has a power that private sorrow cannot match."},
  {q:"What befell the soul that refused to be afflicted on the Day of Atonement?", options:["He would be cut off from his people","He would pay double the atonement money","He would be barred from the feast only","He would fast an extra week"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:29",verse:"For whatsoever soul it be that shall not be afflicted in that same day, he shall be cut off from among his people.",
   insight:"Refusing to humble oneself on the day of mercy was fatal. There is no atonement for the proud who will not bow."},
  {q:"When did the Feast of Tabernacles begin and how long did it last?", options:["The fifteenth day of the seventh month, for seven days","The first day of the eighth month, for three days","The tenth day of the seventh month, for one day","The new moon after harvest, for ten days"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:34",verse:"Speak unto the children of Israel, saying, The fifteenth day of this seventh month shall be the feast of tabernacles for seven days unto the LORD.",
   insight:"The year's final feast was its longest: seven days of dwelling in booths and rejoicing. God ends His calendar with joy."},
  {q:"Who was to dwell in booths during the feast?", options:["All native-born Israelites","Only the priests and Levites","Only the elders of each tribe","Only those who owned vineyards"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 23:42",verse:"Ye shall dwell in booths seven days; all that are Israelites born shall dwell in booths:",
   insight:"Every Israelite family slept under branches for a week. Remembering God's provision was not a priestly specialty; it was a household practice."}
 ]},
{n:24, name:"The Oil, the Bread, and the Blasphemer",
 seal:{ref:"Leviticus 24:22",verse:"Ye shall have one manner of law, as well for the stranger, as for one of your own country: for I am the LORD your God."},
 facts:["Did you know the twelve loaves of showbread were replaced fresh every Sabbath, a weekly reminder that God's provision never goes stale (Leviticus 24:8)?","Did you know the blasphemer's case became the occasion for the eye for eye law, given right after his sentence was pronounced (Leviticus 24:19-20)?","Did you know the lamps in the tabernacle were never to go out? Aaron tended them every evening and morning (Leviticus 24:2-3)."],
 questions:[
  {q:"What did Israel bring for the lamps, and for what purpose?", options:["Pure beaten olive oil, to cause the lamps to burn continually","Wine and spices, to perfume the holy place","Fine flour, to bake the showbread","Gold and silver, to repair the lampstand"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:2",verse:"Command the children of Israel, that they bring unto thee pure oil olive beaten for the light, to cause the lamps to burn continually.",
   insight:"Pure oil, beaten out with labor, kept the light alive without ceasing. Continual light required continual supply, like prayer."},
  {q:"What was Aaron's charge regarding the lamps?", options:["To order them from evening to morning continually before the LORD","To light them only on feast days","To carry them into the most holy place","To let them burn out each Sabbath"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:3",verse:"Without the vail of the testimony, in the tabernacle of the congregation, shall Aaron order it from the evening unto the morning before the LORD continually: it shall be a statute for ever in your generations.",
   insight:"Aaron tended the light every evening and morning. Faithfulness is mostly a matter of showing up at the appointed times."},
  {q:"How many cakes of showbread were baked, and how much flour was in each?", options:["Twelve cakes, two tenth deals of fine flour each","Seven cakes, one tenth deal each","Twelve loaves, a handful each","Two cakes, an ephah each"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:5",verse:"And thou shalt take fine flour, and bake twelve cakes thereof: two tenth deals shall be in one cake.",
   insight:"Twelve loaves for twelve tribes: every tribe had a place at God's table. None was forgotten in His presence."},
  {q:"How were the loaves arranged?", options:["In two rows, six on a row, upon the pure table before the LORD","In a circle around the golden altar","In a single stack of twelve","In four rows of three"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:6",verse:"And thou shalt set them in two rows, six on a row, upon the pure table before the LORD.",
   insight:"Order marked the bread of the Presence. God is not haphazard; even His table setting preached His orderliness."},
  {q:"What was placed on each row of bread, and why?", options:["Pure frankincense, as a memorial offering made by fire","Olive oil, to keep the bread fresh","Salt, as a covenant reminder","Wine, to be poured out"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:7",verse:"And thou shalt put pure frankincense upon each row, that it may be on the bread for a memorial, even an offering made by fire unto the LORD.",
   insight:"Frankincense turned the bread into a memorial before God. Our ordinary provision becomes worship when offered back to Him."},
  {q:"How often was the showbread set in order before the LORD?", options:["Every Sabbath, continually","Every new moon","Once a year at Atonement","Every morning and evening"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:8",verse:"Every sabbath he shall set it in order before the LORD continually, being taken from the children of Israel by an everlasting covenant.",
   insight:"Fresh bread every Sabbath: God's presence never went stale. Weekly renewal keeps fellowship from growing old."},
  {q:"Who ate the showbread, and where?", options:["Aaron and his sons, in the holy place","The whole congregation, in the court","The Levites, at the camp gate","Moses alone, in the tent"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:9",verse:"And it shall be Aaron’s and his sons’; and they shall eat it in the holy place: for it is most holy unto him of the offerings of the LORD made by fire by a perpetual statute.",
   insight:"The bread of God's presence fed His servants in a holy place. Those who serve at His table are sustained from His table."},
  {q:"Who was the man at the center of the blasphemy case?", options:["The son of an Israelite woman and an Egyptian father","An elder of the tribe of Judah","A Levite who guarded the gate","A Midianite merchant in the camp"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:10",verse:"And the son of an Israelitish woman, whose father was an Egyptian, went out among the children of Israel: and this son of the Israelitish woman and a man of Israel strove together in the camp;",
   insight:"His mixed parentage is noted because the law that followed applied to all: stranger and native stood equal before God's name."},
  {q:"What was the blasphemer's mother's name, and of what tribe was she?", options:["Shelomith, daughter of Dibri, of the tribe of Dan","Miriam, daughter of Amram, of Levi","Hannah, daughter of Phanuel, of Asher","Ruth, daughter of Eglon, of Moab"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:11",verse:"And the Israelitish woman’s son blasphemed the name of the LORD, and cursed. And they brought him unto Moses: (and his mother’s name was Shelomith, the daughter of Dibri, of the tribe of Dan:)",
   insight:"Scripture names her fully, refusing to hide the story's shame. God's Word tells the truth about sin, even in Israel's own camp."},
  {q:"What did the man do during the fight in the camp?", options:["He blasphemed the Name of the LORD and cursed","He struck the man of Israel with a stone","He stole from the tabernacle treasury","He refused to keep the Sabbath"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:11",verse:"And the Israelitish woman’s son blasphemed the name of the LORD, and cursed. And they brought him unto Moses: (and his mother’s name was Shelomith, the daughter of Dibri, of the tribe of Dan:)",
   insight:"A quarrel became blasphemy when rage reached for God's name. Anger is dangerous, but anger aimed at God is deadly."},
  {q:"Why was the blasphemer put in custody?", options:["Until the mind of the LORD concerning him was shown","Until witnesses could be found","Until the next Day of Atonement","Until his mother pleaded for him"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:12",verse:"And they put him in ward, that the mind of the LORD might be shewed them.",
   insight:"Even for a clear crime, Moses waited on God. Hasty judgment is not zeal; seeking God's mind first is."},
  {q:"What were the witnesses required to do before the sentence?", options:["Lay their hands upon the blasphemer's head","Wash their hands in the laver","Bring an offering for their testimony","Cover their faces with their mantles"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:14",verse:"Bring forth him that hath cursed without the camp; and let all that heard him lay their hands upon his head, and let all the congregation stone him.",
   insight:"The laying on of hands tied the witnesses to the verdict. Testimony was never casual; it carried weight and consequence."},
  {q:"What sentence did the LORD pronounce on the blasphemer?", options:["The whole congregation would stone him","He would be cut off from the camp forever","He would serve the priests for life","He would pay a ransom of fifty shekels"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:14",verse:"Bring forth him that hath cursed without the camp; and let all that heard him lay their hands upon his head, and let all the congregation stone him.",
   insight:"The whole congregation carried out the sentence, because the whole congregation had heard the Name dishonored. God's honor was the nation's business."},
  {q:"What was the penalty for blaspheming the Name of the LORD?", options:["He would surely be put to death","He would be beaten with forty stripes","He would be sold as a servant","He would be shaven and shamed"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:16",verse:"And he that blasphemeth the name of the LORD, he shall surely be put to death, and all the congregation shall certainly stone him: as well the stranger, as he that is born in the land, when he blasphemeth the name of the LORD, shall be put to death.",
   insight:"God's name is not a swear word. The severity of the penalty teaches the weight of the Name."},
  {q:"Did the blasphemy law apply to foreigners as well?", options:["Yes, stranger and native-born alike were put to death for it","No, only Israelites were bound by it","Only if the stranger lived in the camp","Only on feast days"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:16",verse:"And he that blasphemeth the name of the LORD, he shall surely be put to death, and all the congregation shall certainly stone him: as well the stranger, as he that is born in the land, when he blasphemeth the name of the LORD, shall be put to death.",
   insight:"One law for all: God's name was honored across every boundary. Reverence was not a tribal custom but a universal duty."},
  {q:"What was the penalty for killing a man?", options:["He would surely be put to death","He would flee to a city of refuge always","He would pay the victim's family","He would serve seven years"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:17",verse:"And he that killeth any man shall surely be put to death.",
   insight:"Murder struck at the image of God in man. The law treated life as sacred because the Life-giver is holy."},
  {q:"What restitution was required of one who killed another's animal?", options:["He must make it good, animal for animal","He must pay double its price","He must fast for seven days","He must offer it on the altar"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:18",verse:"And he that killeth a beast shall make it good; beast for beast.",
   insight:"Property mattered to God too. Justice reached down to livestock, teaching that responsibility covers all we handle."},
  {q:"How was a blemish inflicted on another person repaid?", options:["Breach for breach, eye for eye, tooth for tooth","With a fine set by the elders","With double the value in silver","With exile from the camp"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:20",verse:"Breach for breach, eye for eye, tooth for tooth: as he hath caused a blemish in a man, so shall it be done to him again.",
   insight:"Eye for eye limited revenge; it did not license it. Justice was to be measured, never multiplied by rage."},
  {q:"What did God require regarding the stranger and the citizen?", options:["One manner of law for both","Stricter laws for the stranger","Lighter laws for the citizen","Separate courts for each"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:22",verse:"Ye shall have one manner of law, as well for the stranger, as for one of your own country: for I am the LORD your God.",
   insight:"Justice wore no favoritism. The foreigner got the same law as the native, because God's righteousness shows no partiality."},
  {q:"What became of the blasphemer in the end?", options:["The children of Israel stoned him, as the LORD commanded Moses","He was released for lack of witnesses","He fled to Egypt with his father","He was pardoned on the Day of Atonement"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 24:23",verse:"And Moses spake to the children of Israel, that they should bring forth him that had cursed out of the camp, and stone him with stones. And the children of Israel did as the LORD commanded Moses.",
   insight:"Israel did as the LORD commanded, even when it was hard. Obedience is proved not in easy commands but in costly ones."}
 ]},
{n:25, name:"The Sabbath Year and Jubilee",
 seal:{ref:"Leviticus 25:10",verse:"And ye shall hallow the fiftieth year, and proclaim liberty throughout all the land unto all the inhabitants thereof: it shall be a jubile unto you; and ye shall return every man unto his possession, and ye shall return every man unto his family."},
 facts:["Did you know the Jubilee trumpet sounded on the Day of Atonement, tying national freedom to the day of forgiveness (Leviticus 25:9)?","Did you know land prices in Israel were really lease prices? Every sale was priced by the harvests remaining until Jubilee (Leviticus 25:15-16).","Did you know God promised the sixth year's harvest would feed Israel for three years, covering the sabbath year and the year after (Leviticus 25:21)?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"What was the land itself to keep when Israel entered Canaan?", options:["A sabbath unto the LORD","A year of war","A census of the tribes","A wall around the cities"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:2",verse:"Speak unto the children of Israel, and say unto them, When ye come into the land which I give you, then shall the land keep a sabbath unto the LORD.",
   insight:"Even the soil belonged to God's rhythm. Rest was woven into creation, not just into the calendar."},
  {q:"What was forbidden in the seventh year?", options:["Sowing the field and pruning the vineyard","Building houses and digging wells","Marrying and giving in marriage","Trading in the markets"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:4",verse:"But in the seventh year shall be a sabbath of rest unto the land, a sabbath for the LORD: thou shalt neither sow thy field, nor prune thy vineyard.",
   insight:"For a whole year, Israel lived off God's stored provision. The sabbath year was a school of trust."},
  {q:"What could not be reaped in the sabbath year?", options:["What grew of its own accord from the harvest","The grapes of the vineyard","The olives of the grove","The wheat of the field's edge"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:5",verse:"That which groweth of its own accord of thy harvest thou shalt not reap, neither gather the grapes of thy vine undressed: for it is a year of rest unto the land.",
   insight:"Volunteer growth was left for the needy, not harvested for profit. Rest years turned owners into sharers."},
  {q:"Who might eat the sabbath year's produce?", options:["The household, servants, hired workers, and the stranger","Only the priests and their families","Only the landowner and his sons","Only the poor of the land"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:6",verse:"And the sabbath of the land shall be meat for you; for thee, and for thy servant, and for thy maid, and for thy hired servant, and for thy stranger that sojourneth with thee,",
   insight:"The rest year's bounty was common grace: master and servant, citizen and stranger ate alike. God's provision levels our ranks."},
  {q:"How many years made up seven sabbaths of years?", options:["Forty-nine years","Fifty years","Seventy years","Thirty-five years"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:8",verse:"And thou shalt number seven sabbaths of years unto thee, seven times seven years; and the space of the seven sabbaths of years shall be unto thee forty and nine years.",
   insight:"God counted in sevens, and taught Israel to count with Him. Sacred arithmetic trained them to think in His patterns."},
  {q:"When was the trumpet of the Jubilee sounded?", options:["On the tenth day of the seventh month, the Day of Atonement","On the first day of the first month","At the Feast of Weeks","On the Sabbath of Sabbaths"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:9",verse:"Then shalt thou cause the trumpet of the jubile to sound on the tenth day of the seventh month, in the day of atonement shall ye make the trumpet sound throughout all your land.",
   insight:"Liberty was proclaimed on the day of forgiveness. Freedom and atonement have always sounded together."},
  {q:"What was proclaimed in the fiftieth year?", options:["Liberty throughout all the land to all its inhabitants","War against the surrounding nations","A new king over Israel","A census of every tribe"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:10",verse:"And ye shall hallow the fiftieth year, and proclaim liberty throughout all the land unto all the inhabitants thereof: it shall be a jubile unto you; and ye shall return every man unto his possession, and ye shall return every man unto his family.",
   insight:"Jubilee was good news announced with a trumpet. The gospel still sounds like this: liberty proclaimed to all."},
  {q:"What two homecomings did the Jubilee bring?", options:["Every man to his possession and every man to his family","Every soldier to his city and every priest to the altar","Every debtor to the temple and every servant to the field","Every tribe to its banner and every elder to the gate"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:10",verse:"And ye shall hallow the fiftieth year, and proclaim liberty throughout all the land unto all the inhabitants thereof: it shall be a jubile unto you; and ye shall return every man unto his possession, and ye shall return every man unto his family.",
   insight:"Jubilee restored both land and kin. God's redemption gives back what life took: place and people."},
  {q:"How was the price of land set between Jubilees?", options:["According to the number of years remaining until the Jubilee","By the size of the field in acres","By the age of the buyer","By decree of the elders each year"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:15",verse:"According to the number of years after the jubile thou shalt buy of thy neighbour, and according unto the number of years of the fruits he shall sell unto thee:",
   insight:"Every sale was really a lease with God's expiry date. Israel never owned the land outright; they rented from the true Landlord."},
  {q:"How did the years remaining affect the price?", options:["More years meant a higher price; fewer years, a lower price","The price stayed fixed by law","Older buyers paid less","The price doubled every seven years"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:16",verse:"According to the multitude of years thou shalt increase the price thereof, and according to the fewness of years thou shalt diminish the price of it: for according to the number of the years of the fruits doth he sell unto thee.",
   insight:"Honest pricing honored the Jubilee. Fair dealing was worship, and cheating a neighbor was defying God."},
  {q:"What attitude was commanded in buying and selling?", options:["Do not oppress one another, but fear God","Drive the hardest bargain possible","Sell only to your own tribe","Never sell at a loss"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:17",verse:"Ye shall not therefore oppress one another; but thou shalt fear thy God: for I am the LORD your God.",
   insight:"The fear of God was the foundation of fair trade. Business ethics began at the altar, not the marketplace."},
  {q:"What did God promise for the sixth year?", options:["A blessing bringing forth fruit for three years","Rain in every season","Double the flocks and herds","Peace from all enemies"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:21",verse:"Then I will command my blessing upon you in the sixth year, and it shall bring forth fruit for three years.",
   insight:"God underwrote the sabbath year in advance. Obedience that looks risky is safe when God has promised the supply."},
  {q:"Why could the land never be sold permanently?", options:["The land was God's; Israel were strangers and sojourners with Him","The tribes would quarrel over borders","The priests needed the tithe","The Jubilee came too often"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:23",verse:"The land shall not be sold for ever: for the land is mine; for ye are strangers and sojourners with me.",
   insight:"Israel owned nothing outright; they were tenants of God. Remembering we are sojourners keeps our grip on possessions loose."},
  {q:"Who could redeem a poor brother's sold land?", options:["A near kinsman","Any willing stranger","Only the high priest","The elders of the city"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:25",verse:"If thy brother be waxen poor, and hath sold away some of his possession, and if any of his kin come to redeem it, then shall he redeem that which his brother sold.",
   insight:"Redemption was a family duty. God wove rescue into kinship, a shadow of the Kinsman who would redeem us all."},
  {q:"How long did redemption last for a house sold in a walled city?", options:["Within a whole year after it was sold","Until the next Jubilee","For seven full years","Forever, at any time"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:29",verse:"And if a man sell a dwelling house in a walled city, then he may redeem it within a whole year after it is sold; within a full year may he redeem it.",
   insight:"City houses followed different rules than fields, yet mercy still had its window. God's law was detailed because life is detailed."},
  {q:"How were houses in unwalled villages treated?", options:["As fields of the country: redeemable, and released in the Jubilee","As walled-city houses, with one year only","As holy property, never sold","As common land, owned by all"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:31",verse:"But the houses of the villages which have no wall round about them shall be counted as the fields of the country: they may be redeemed, and they shall go out in the jubile.",
   insight:"Village homes shared the land's redemption. Where people lived close to the soil, grace reached their doorsteps too."},
  {q:"What special redemption right did the Levites hold?", options:["They could redeem their houses at any time","They could never sell at all","They paid half price always","They redeemed only in the Jubilee"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:32",verse:"Notwithstanding the cities of the Levites, and the houses of the cities of their possession, may the Levites redeem at any time.",
   insight:"The Levites, who owned no farmland, were guarded in what little they had. God protects the portion of those who serve Him."},
  {q:"What was forbidden in lending to a poor brother?", options:["Taking usury or interest from him","Lending him more than he asked","Requiring a pledge at all","Lending without witnesses"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:36",verse:"Take thou no usury of him, or increase: but fear thy God; that thy brother may live with thee.",
   insight:"Profiting from a brother's poverty was forbidden. Love does not monetize misery."},
  {q:"How must a master not rule over his Israelite servant?", options:["Not with rigour, with harshness","Not with kindness, lest he grow lazy","Not with wages, but with food only","Not with words, but with the rod"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:43",verse:"Thou shalt not rule over him with rigour; but shalt fear thy God.",
   insight:"Even lawful authority had limits. Power without gentleness is tyranny, and God forbade it in His people's homes."},
  {q:"How long did the Israelite servant serve his master?", options:["Until the year of Jubilee","For seven years only","For the rest of his life","Until the next Passover"], a:0,
   ref:"Leviticus 25:40",verse:"But as an hired servant, and as a sojourner, he shall be with thee, and shall serve thee unto the year of jubile:",
   insight:"No Israelite's bondage was permanent. Every servant's calendar had a liberation day circled by God."}
 ]}
]);
